\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ezekiel 34 opens with prophesy against the shepherds of Israel. Ezekiel so far has threatened judgement many ways against Judah and the nations, so in a way this is recovering ground, as Ezekiel so often does.\
And yet, something is different this time. For one, where the oracle is positioned in the larger book - after the oracles against the nations, after chapter 33, with oracles promising restoration following.\
For another, something about how Ezekiel is using Leviticus has shifted. It is not so much that repentance is no longer the goal, but that the blessing conditional on righteousness and repentance, or else cursing, is no longer the emphasis.
