\f1 . Ezekiel 34 has no 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1  anywhere. The entire blessing sequence hangs on first-person divine verbs \'97 I will seek, I will rescue, I will feed, I will make a covenant, I will send down rain. The condition hasn't been softened; it has been 
\i absorbed
\i0 . YHWH takes over the role whose failure triggered the curses.\
And here's the bold claim I'd stake: the mechanism of the shift is that chapter 34 diagnoses the covenant failure as a 
\i shepherding
\i0  failure, and then YHWH answers it by becoming the shepherd himself. The curses of Leviticus 26 fell \'97 the flock scattered (
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1514 \u1456 \u1468 \u1508 \u1493 \u1468 \u1510 \u1462 \u1497 \u1504 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 , vv. 5-6, echoing the scattering curses of Lev 26:33) \'97 but the indictment here lands on the shepherds, not the flock. So the restoration doesn't run through the flock's repentance meeting the condition; it runs through the removal of failed mediators and their replacement by YHWH-plus-David. 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 \u1470 \u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1512 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  (v. 11) \'97 the doubled pronoun is emphatic self-substitution. The sheep were 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1497 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1461 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1461 \u1497 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1489 \u1463 \u1511 \u1461 \u1468 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  (v. 6); now the seeking verbs return with 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  as subject (v. 16 reverses v. 4 item by item, in mirrored order).\
This is why the placement matters as you say: after chapter 33 established that turning is possible and pleaded for it, chapter 34 reveals that the turning will be 
\i effected
\i0 , not merely awaited. The new-heart logic of 36:26 is already structurally present here.\
One complication worth pressing, though: the conditional isn't wholly gone. Verses 17-22 introduce judgment 
\i within
\i0  the flock \'97 between fat and lean sheep, rams and goats. So the unconditional covenant of peace coexists with continued discrimination. The condition has moved location: no longer "obey and be blessed," but "the flock will be sorted as it is gathered." Even 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1491 
\f1  against the fat and strong (v. 16). Grace toward the flock as such; judgment still operative between sheep. That tension \'97 sworn restoration plus internal sorting \'97 seems to me the actual theological structure of the chapter, and it guards against reading 34 as pure unconditionality.
