\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I think you present excellent evidence for much of what you say. But not on a few points.\
I would not go so far as to say that the chapter diagnoses the covenant failure as a shepherding failure. Ezekiel offers many overlapping diagnoses elsewhere, and he is not at all shy of being quite clear with a therefore or the like, making explicit that a particular kind of wrongdoing led to covenant breach and judgement. There is clearly shepherding failure. But to know what part that failure plays in the rhetoric of this oracle, we first need to determine who the shepherds even are. Kings of Judah? Leadership or elites more broadly? And then, we need to determine why Ezekiel brings them and their failure up as he does.\
Moreover, while turning effected by judgement was a theme of many of the earlier oracles against Judah, and new hearts will come in a later oracle, where is it mentioned in this one? Ezekiel brings up such things so many times so many ways. But not here. That is part of the evidence before us, and speaking as if turning and new heart is implicit or structurally present might make us think we have addressed that evidence better than we have.\
Lastly, is the implied conditional the reason why Ezekiel brings up judging between fat sheep and lean sheep? Where is the evidence for this? How does that judging fit into the larger rhetorical sweep of what Ezekiel is doing here?
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
