\b \cf2 Who are the shepherds?
\b0  The evidence points to kings, but the plural matters. The singular resolution \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1489 \u1456 \u1491 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1493 \u1460 \u1491 
\f1  as 
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1465 \u1506 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1495 \u1464 \u1491 
\f1 , a 
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1488 
\f1  (v. 23-24) \'97 answers a royal failure with a royal replacement; you don't fix failed priests or prophets by appointing a Davidic prince. Jeremiah 23:1-6, the closest parallel oracle, moves identically from 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1465 \u1506 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  to a Davidic branch, and there the shepherds are unambiguously the royal house. And Ezekiel has already run the sequence of Judah's last kings as doomed animals in chapter 19. But note: chapter 22 indicted princes, priests, prophets, and people of the land 
\i separately and by title
\i0 . Here he refuses titles \'97 just 
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1465 \u1506 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1 . So the target is royal, but the presentation is deliberately functional rather than titular: they are named by the office they failed to perform, not the office they held. That choice is data.\

\b Why raise them here, in this position?
\b0  Here's my revised claim: the shepherd indictment is not offered as diagnosis of the covenant breach \'97 you're right, there's no 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1 ... 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1499 \u1461 \u1503 
\f1  tracing exile to shepherd-sin as its cause; the 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1499 \u1461 \u1503 
\f1  of vv. 7-10 issues in the shepherds' 
\i removal from office
\i0 , not in Judah's fall. Rather, the oracle is answering the question chapter 33 left standing: the city has fallen, the survivors say 
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1463 \u1495 \u1456 \u1504 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1511 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  \'97 if restoration comes, 
\i through what agency?
\i0  Every human mediating structure has been judged. The rhetorical function of vv. 1-10 is to eliminate the last candidate. The flock cannot be restored by better shepherds of the same kind, because the failure mode \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1510 \u1465 \u1468 \u1488 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1460 \u1512 \u1456 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1 , feeding themselves 
\i from
\i0  the flock \'97 is presented as what those shepherds do. Verse 8's structure is telling: the doubled 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1468 \u1512 \u1456 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1512 \u1465 \u1506 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  ("the shepherds fed 
\i themselves
\i0 ") against 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1510 \u1465 \u1488 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1464 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1 . The indictment exists to clear the ground for 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 \u1470 \u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 . It's the same move as chapters 29-32 stripping Egypt as an object of trust \'97 but now applied to Israel's own internal leadership. The oracle's center of gravity is v. 11 onward; vv. 1-10 are demolition to make that arrival intelligible.\

\b On turning and new hearts: withdrawn, and the silence is sharper than I treated it.
\b0  Not one verb in this chapter has the flock as subject of a moral action. The sheep stray, scatter, are eaten, are trampled \'97 passive or pitiable throughout. No 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1 , no repentance sought, no response required even after rescue. Chapter 33 ended with a plea for turning; chapter 34 doesn't repeat the plea, it changes the actor. That's not "new heart implicit" \'97 it's something more radical and more specific: restoration presented entirely without a human hinge. The recognition formulas (vv. 27, 30) come 
\i after
\i0  deliverance, as its effect, not its condition. If anything, the chapter's anthropology is bleaker than the new-heart oracle: sheep don't repent. They get sought.\

\b The fat/lean judgment, re-read without the conditional frame.
\b0  Look at what vv. 18-19 actually accuse: trampling the pasture, fouling the water \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1511 \u1463 \u1506 
\f1 ... 
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1512 \u1456 \u1508 \u1465 \u1468 \u1513 \u1474 \u1493 \u1468 \u1503 
\f1 . This is the 
\i same crime
\i0  as the shepherds' in kind: consuming in a way that destroys what others must live on. And v. 21, shoving with flank and shoulder, scattering the weak 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1495 \u1493 \u1468 \u1510 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  \'97 the fat sheep 
\i cause the scattering
\i0 , the very condition vv. 5-6 attributed to shepherdlessness. So the rhetorical function I'd now argue: vv. 17-22 close a loophole in the oracle's own logic. If the diagnosis were only "bad shepherds," the flock could hear vv. 1-10 as vindication \'97 we were victims. The sorting within the flock blocks that reception: the predatory pattern is not confined to office-holders; it reproduces at every scale where strength meets weakness. Deposing shepherds doesn't end predation, because some sheep shepherd themselves at their neighbors' expense. Which is why YHWH's shepherding must include 
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1508 \u1463 \u1496 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1461 \u1468 \u1497 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1462 \u1474 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1513 \u1462 \u1474 \u1492 
\f1  \'97 rescue 
\i of
\i0  the flock requires judgment 
\i within
\i0  the flock, or the rescue just delivers the weak to new predators inside the fold.\
That reading also explains why v. 16 ends 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1491 
\f1 ... 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1512 \u1456 \u1506 \u1462 \u1504 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 
\f1  right at the seam: the shepherd's care and the shepherd's judgment are one activity, not a blessing track plus a condition track. The chapter isn't unconditional-covenant-with-an-asterisk. It's a portrait of what shepherding 
\i is
\i0  when done rightly \'97 and it includes the knife.
