\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 On who are the shepherds, I would firm up your conclusion by noting that the judgement between sheep would be somewhat incoherent were the shepherds merely elite. Additionally, building on what you said, Ezekiel has a long history of refusing titles specifically to the kings of Judah.\
On why raise them here, I do think you are correct, Ezekiel has been eliminating false hopes for several chapters. That is further evidence you are correct that the rhetorical function here is to eliminate the last candidate, or at least, the last candidate in which the people put their hope. This is tricky surgery, because the people have falsely been putting their hope in the Davidic monarchy and in God's unconditional blessing of his people, and the true answer is a Davidic king and God's unconditional blessing of his people. But first, this is a claim that needs evidence, and second, the search for that evidence will tell us much about how Ezekiel manages that surgery, if that is what he is doing.\
As for the fat/lean judgement, there are similarities in the charges with those against the shepherds. But also differences.\
And I would like to make claims myself as to why. First, judging some of you to bring peace recasts the massive judgement that just happened. Second, chapter 34 is just as much against despair as 33, against lack of faith, against lack of repentance as before, but also reconciling restoration with all the reasons before for punishment. That reconciliation is necessary because, as in 33, the LORD's ways must not be seen as arbitrary. And so, the restoration too must not be seen as arbitrary. And that makes it the more sure, which is also part of the message.
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
