\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Peace, we are definitely going to have to get back to once we work out the earlier parts of the oracle in detail.\
Which for the moment means looking at the lack in the charges against the fat sheep. Strictly, Torah put various positive duties on ordinary people. They might be redeemers, lenders, or owners of fields who could permit gleaning, for instance. Still, in the face of the human equivalents of pasture stomping, water muddying, and weak attacking, focusing on the negative seems understandable enough. But the verbs used are once, destroy, and multiple times, judge.\
Compare to the charges against the kings. In the analogy, the owner will require the sheep - not unlike Genesis 9, requiring a reckoning for blood shed, for going against the image of God in one's fellow man. The word used of requiring blood of the watchman in chapter 33, but also the word for searching in verses 6 and 8.\
Notice also that the judgement against the fat sheep comes out the LORD caring for his flock as a whole. To be the shepherd means to be the judge over the flock. To feed the flock means to deal with the ones marring the grass. To bind up the injured is to deal with causes of injury, foreign and domestic.
