\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Yes, judgement and providing justice had long been understood as a function of good kings - for that is what shepherds are in this chapter. A good king provides justice for his people, putting a stop to oppression whether from outside or inside. The LORD is claiming to be just such a king. Ezekiel has brought up such shepherding by the LORD in 11:17, though this is not a new idea here in Ezekiel. Micah 2 takes up the analogy of the LORD as shepherd in the face of domestic injustice. Micah 4, in the face of the nations. And David himself had used the analogy in Psalm 23, the righteous king being under the rule and care of his own shepherd. As for the attack on unrighteous shepherds, and replacement with the LORD's servant, David, Jeremiah 23 had used the LORD as shepherd gathering his sheep before.\
But also, kings as shepherds, even bringing back strays, was a common metaphor in that time and place. And indeed, bringing back strays is a core part of the job of a literal shepherd; sheep stray. And it is not like the exile is not very much in the minds of Ezekiel's audience.\
As for the word in verse 16, which I translated destroy, yes, various translators of the Masoretic Text have translated differently, which has led some to suggest that an original d was flipped to r by the time of the Masoretic Text. But in addition to your arguments, I note that Ezekiel used that same word for the judgement against false prophets in 14:9, and Deuteronomy used the word as part of the covenant curses.
