\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I know I brought up the Deuteronomy use of destruction. But do we have evidence that destruction is being used in a covenant sanction sort of way here? For instance, when 14:9 uses the word, the reason for the destruction and punishment is made explicit - that the house of Israel might not go astray or defile themselves, but be the LORD's people. That resembles chapter 34 more than it does covenant curses. But perhaps there is evidence elsewhere in chapter 34 for a covenant curse interpretation?\
I do think you may be on to something though with this chapter using the standard the nations all claimed for their kings.\
Moreover, the Psalm 23 template, in a way, is as old as humanity. When we were created, God gave us dominion, including over literal sheep. But also, commands. And his presence with us. This, too, we need to carry into our discussion of peace when we get there, including in verse 31.\
But what we are dancing around with Micah and Jeremiah, and with our earlier discussion, we need to cover more explicitly. Why David? What exactly is Ezekiel doing bringing up one shepherd?
