\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Now, it is true that in appointing a single shepherd, the LORD aims at the division of the kingdom. I add, as evidence, the verbal similarity between this chapter, and Jeremiah 30, which explicitly mentions Israel and Judah.\
But to say only that is to skip over the language surrounding that single shepherd. I myself will judge. I will judge. I will raise over them. A dynasty can pretend, falsely, that it continues by simple biological and political succession, not by the LORD's appointment. Think of the message of many earlier chapters of Ezekiel, and of this chapter, the LORD's intention to act, himself.\
Moreover, you mention David as servant, but then you speak of David as shepherd - which is not wrong, but think of all the time this chapter spent speaking of the self serving nature of the earlier kings. Meanwhile, the LORD repeatedly referred to David as his servant, as he had Moses. And some of that relationship comes out in the original Davidic covenant.\
Some of that is, of course, the submission to the LORD you spoke of. But being specially raised up, being the LORD's servant, also speaks of a high position in a way.\
It may be that adam means only human, in verse 31. But between one shepherd with high position, and being the LORD's sheep, the sheep of the LORD's pasture, being a human flock is a high position in this case as well.\
Now, though more perhaps needs said about exactly how Ezekiel is bringing together all these pieces of rhetoric, it is time to ask what Ezekiel intends to communicate by this covenant of peace. What even is the definition of peace here.
