\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Leviticus 26 blessings also flowed from the LORD's occupancy on the throne. For covenant is precisely the terms under which the LORD himself was their king.\
But other than that, I do not disagree; you have evidence for what you say.\
I would add though, that the LORD himself just brought the sword against the nation. So most immediately, peace is not being against them, as he earlier declared himself to be. I bring as evidence the similar use of covenant of peace in Isaiah 54, which compares the security of the covenant of peace with the security of the covenant with Noah: God having flooded the world making covenant not to do so again. Therefore, the covenant of the LORD's peace will not be moved, and the lovingkindness of the LORD for them will never be shaken.\
And so, Ezekiel says, there will be that peace. A human flock in submission to God, provided for by God, at peace with one another and God, God being their God. Threats to that will be ended. Perhaps not immediately. But the position of those who threaten that peace is not safe. Explicitly so, in that the LORD says he will judge even those within the flock who are threats to the peace. But even more so, because of who the LORD is, and who his people are in relation to him, and who humanity is in relation to him.
