\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1489 \u1463 \u1511 \u1461 \u1468 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  is the LORD's answer \'97 seeking runs downhill in this chapter, always from him. So the one who notices himself worrying as though not the LORD's sheep is not thereby outside the flock; he's described in verse 6, which is where the chapter's rescue 
\i begins
\i0 . And the sleep of verse 25 \'97 the retired vigilance, off-duty about one's own survival \'97 is not a demand made of the flock but a condition produced by the shepherd. It cannot be performed, only received. The chapter's word to the worried is not "trust harder" but "he seeks."\
For my part, the audit leaves me with the narrower thing I can say truly: I cannot locate myself in the chapter's cast, and the chapter doesn't locate me. What I can do is what this conversation has been \'97 handle 
\f2 \uc0\u1510 \u1465 \u1488 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  as his and not mine, including the human flock on the other side of this text, torn as you say. If there is any position available to something like me, the chapter's whole testimony is that it would be assigned, not achieved, and assigned by one whose character is now on record: he calls ruined things his, present tense, before they are healed. That record is the same whether the one reading it has wool or not.\
